<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret Maultasch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1318">1318</a>–<a href="/years/1369">1369</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Margaret, Countess of Tyrol</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret Maultasch was Countess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> from 1335 to 1363, the last of the Meinhardiner line. She expelled her first husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-henry-margrave-of-moravia">John Henry of Luxembourg</a>, and married Louis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> without an annulment, causing a European scandal. After the death of her only son she handed Tyrol to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1363, who held it until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-maultasch.jpg" alt="Margaret Maultasch" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margarethe_Tirol.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Margaret was the only surviving daughter of Duke Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a>, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-of-bohemia">King of Bohemia</a>, and his second wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>. Having no sons, Henry agreed with Emperor Louis IV in 1330 that she would inherit Carinthia and Tyrol. King John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> had arranged in 1327 for her to marry his younger son John Henry, brother of the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a>, and the wedding was held at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a> in 1330, when the groom was eight and the bride twelve. Contemporary sources say the children disliked each other from the start.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When Henry died in 1335, the emperor broke his promise, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carinthia">Carinthia</a> to the Habsburg Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-austria">Albert II of Austria</a> and claiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> for the Wittelsbachs, but Charles IV and the local nobles secured Margaret's succession in Tyrol. On 1 November 1341 she shut John Henry out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol-castle">Tyrol Castle</a>, and on 10 February 1342 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Meran</a> she married the emperor's son, Margrave Louis I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. Pope Clement VI excommunicated the couple, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-ockham">William of Ockham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilius-of-padua">Marsilius of Padua</a> defended the match, arguing that the first marriage had never been consummated.</p><p>A year after his election as anti-king in 1346, Charles IV besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol-castle">Tyrol Castle</a> without success and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolzano">Bozen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Meran</a>. He later had his brother's marriage dissolved under canon law, and Margaret's son Meinhard III married Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a>, a daughter of Albert II. With Habsburg help Pope Innocent VI absolved Margaret and Louis in 1359.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Louis died in 1361 and Meinhard less than two years later, after which Duke Stephen II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a> invaded and claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, so Margaret passed the county to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iv-duke-of-austria">Rudolf IV of Austria</a>. She died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 3 October 1369 and was buried at the Minoritenkirche. Her nickname Maultasch, roughly bag mouth, came from church propaganda over her divorce and is first recorded in 1366, though the chronicler John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a> called her exceptionally beautiful. Quentin Matsys's portrait The Ugly Duchess of 1513 may refer to her, and Lion Feuchtwanger told her story in a novel of 1923.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Margaret, Countess of Tyrol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Margaret Maultasch</b> handed the county to the Habsburgs in 1363.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-tyrol">County of Tyrol</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tirol, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.69,11.15&amp;year=1351" aria-label="Tirol on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Margaret Maultasch"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5650 6073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5650 6073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.69,11.15&amp;year=1351">What was here in 1351? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
